A <Sierpiński set> is an uncountable <subset> $S$ of $\mathbb R$ such that $S\cap N$ is countable for every <set> $N$ of <Lebesgue measure> zero. Equivalently it is enough to test <Borel null sets>, because every <null set> is contained in a Borel <null set>. **Uncountability and countable intersection with every <null set> are both required.** This is the measure analogue of the category-based <Luzin set> condition; it does not say that $S$ itself is measurable.

