= Solution
{parent=a}

Let $f$ be given and choose $Y\subseteq\mathbb R$ of <cardinality> $\aleph_1$. Each $f(y)$ is countable, so the <axiom of choice> and <infinite cardinal arithmetic> give
$$
\left|Y\cup\bigcup_{y\in Y}f(y)\right|\le\aleph_1.
$$
Since the continuum is larger, choose $x$ outside this union. Then $x\notin f(y)$ for every $y\in Y$. The countable <set> $f(x)$ cannot contain $Y$, so choose $y\in Y\setminus f(x)$. Thus
$$
\boxed{x\notin f(y),\qquad y\notin f(x).}
$$
The two points are distinct because $x\notin Y$. This proves the <countable-valued free-pair criterion> and the required direction of the <Freiling axiom of symmetry> without any measurability assumption on $f$.

= b
{parent=ii}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Assume the <continuum hypothesis> and enumerate $\mathbb R=\{r_\alpha:\alpha<\omega_1\}$. Define
$$
f(r_\alpha)=\{r_\beta:\beta\le\alpha\}.
$$
Every value is countable. For any two indices, say $\alpha\le\beta$, one has $r_\alpha\in f(r_\beta)$, so the two required nonmembership conditions cannot both hold. Including the endpoint in each initial segment also rules out taking the two points equal. Therefore the free-pair assertion implies the negation of <Continuum hypothesis>. <Cantor theorem> and choice already give $2^{\aleph_0}\ge\aleph_1$, so
$$
\boxed{A_{<\aleph_1}(\mathbb R)\ \Longleftrightarrow\ 2^{\aleph_0}>\aleph_1\quad\text{in ZFC}.}
$$

= c
{parent=ii}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The <Gödel constructible universe theorem> gives $\operatorname{Con}(\mathrm{ZFC})\Rightarrow\operatorname{Con}(\mathrm{ZFC}+\mathrm{CH})$. The <forcing> independence theorem for the <continuum hypothesis> gives $\operatorname{Con}(\mathrm{ZFC})\Rightarrow\operatorname{Con}(\mathrm{ZFC}+\neg\mathrm{CH})$. For example, the latter can be obtained by first passing to the <constructible universe> and then adding sufficiently many Cohen reals. By the equivalence in part (b), these are respectively models of the negation and affirmation of the free-pair assertion. Thus **if <ZFC> is consistent, the assertion is independent of <ZFC>**. The consistency qualification is essential: an inconsistent theory proves every sentence. These are syntactic relative-consistency implications, not a claim that bare consistency supplies a <countable transitive model>.

= iii
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The <axiom of infinity> excludes $V_\omega$ and all smaller stages, so the <cardinal> $\lambda$ is uncountable. <Full semantics for second-order logic> is decisive: every externally specified functional relation whose <ordered pairs> lie in $V_\lambda$ is an allowed class parameter, even if the relation itself is not an element of $V_\lambda$.

If $\operatorname{cf}(\lambda)=\mu<\lambda$, choose an external cofinal <function> $h:\mu\to\lambda$. Its graph is an allowed class parameter: each input, output and <ordered pair> has <rank of a set> below the infinite <cardinal> $\lambda$, which is a <limit ordinal>. The domain $\mu$ belongs to $V_\lambda$. The second-order form of <axiom schema of replacement> would give its range as an element of $V_\lambda$. But a cofinal <subset> of $\lambda$ has <rank of a set> $\lambda$ and cannot belong to $V_\lambda$. Therefore $\operatorname{cf}(\lambda)=\lambda$.

If $2^\theta\ge\lambda$ for a <cardinal> $\theta<\lambda$, choice supplies an external <surjection> $q:\mathcal P(\theta)\to\lambda$. The domain belongs to $V_\lambda$ and the <ordered pairs> of its graph have ranks below $\lambda$. Applying <axiom schema of replacement> to this external functional class would put its range, the <ordinal> $\lambda$, in $V_\lambda$, again impossible. Hence $2^\theta<\lambda$ for every $\theta<\lambda$.

Consequently
$$
\boxed{\lambda>\aleph_0,\qquad \operatorname{cf}(\lambda)=\lambda,\qquad
\forall\theta<\lambda\ (2^\theta<\lambda).}
$$
These are exactly the defining conditions for a <strongly inaccessible cardinal>. This <full second-order replacement rank obstruction> would not follow from <Henkin semantics>, where the available class relations may exclude the external <functions> just used.

